    "chunk_text": "\u0d85\u0dd9\u0dcf\u0dad\u0dba\u0dd4\u0dd9\u0dcf\u0d9c\u0dda \u0db4\u0dca\u200d\u0dbb\u0d9a\u0dcf\u0dc1\u0dca\u0dba \u0b9a\u0baa\u0bbe\u0ba4\u0bc1 \u0bae\u0b95\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bc8\u0bbf\u0ba9\u0bcd \u0b9a\u0bc1\u0b95\u0bbe\u0ba4\u0bbe\u0bb0\u0ba4\u0bcd\u0ba4\u0bc1\u0b95\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0baa\u0bc1\u0b95\u0b95\u0baf\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc8\u0baf\u0bbe\u0bb2\u0bcd \u0b8f\u0bb1\u0bcd\u0baa\u0b9f\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bcd \u0baa\u0bbe\u0ba4\u0bbf\u0baa\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bcd \u0b9a\u0b9f\u0bcd\u0b9f\u0bc8\u0bbf\u0b9a\u0bb0\u0bbe\u0ba4 \u0baa\u0bc1\u0b95\u0b95\u0baf\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc8 \u0bc8\u0bb0\u0bcd\u0ba4\u0bcd\u0ba4\u0b95\u0ba4\u0bcd\u0ba4\u0bbe\u0bb2\u0bcd \u0b85\u0bb0\u0b9a \u0bc8\u0bb0\u0bc1\u0bc8\u0bbe\u0baf\u0bcd \u0b87\u0bb4\u0baa\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bcd: \u0ba8\u0bbf\u0ba4\u0bbf, \u0ba4\u0bbf\u0b9f\u0bcd\u0b9f\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b9f\u0bb2\u0bcd\n\n\u0baa\u0bbf\u0bb0\u0ba4\u0bbf \u0b85\u0b95\u0bae\u0b9a\u0bcd\u0b9a\u0bbe\u0bbf\u0ba9\u0ba4\u0bc1 \u0b95\u0bc2\u0bb1\u0bcd\u0bb1\u0bc1 IMPACT OF TOBACCO ON PUBLIC HEALTH AND\n\nLOSS OF GOVERNMENT REVENUE DUE TO ILLICIT TOBACCO TRADE: STATEMENT BY DEPUTY MINISTER OF FINANCE AND PLANNING\n\n1. The Global Adult Tobacco Survey - GATS - Sri Lanka 2020 Report, published by the Ministry of Health, estimates 24,461 smoking-attributable deaths in 2019, while the Annual Trend Survey on Tobacco Use in Sri Lanka 2024, published by the Alcohol and Drug Information Centre - ADIC - estimates approximately 18,250 smoking-related deaths annually. Accordingly, the available estimates indicate that tobacco use is associated with more than 20,000 deaths annually in Sri Lanka.\n\n2. The Government recognizes that tobacco consumption imposes significant economic and healthcare costs on society. However, the total cost varies depending on the methodology and assumptions used and therefore, no single official estimate has been adopted, as of now.\n\nOn the other hand, tobacco taxation remains an important policy tool to discourage tobacco consumption while generating Government revenue. The revenue collected from tobacco-related taxes, both indirect and corporate taxes, during the year 2025 amounted to Rs. 157,061 million, that is Rs. 157 billion.\n\n3. The annual cigarette consumption declined significantly by 53.1 per cent from approximately 4.1 billion sticks in 2015 to 1.923 billion sticks in 2025. Over the same period, the Government revenue from Excise Duty on cigarettes and Tobacco Tax increased from Rs. 81 billion in 2015 to Rs. 102.7 billion in 2025.\n\nIn addition to the Excise Duty and the Tobacco Tax, cigarettes are subject to the Value Added Tax and a Social Security Contribution Levy of 2.5 per"
