    "chunk_text": "The illegal importation of cigarettes constitutes an offence under the Customs Ordinance and the Tobacco Tax Act. In Sri Lanka, the Customs take necessary action to detect and seize such illicit imports at the country\u2019s airports and seaports and to initiate legal proceedings against the persons involved in such offences. In addition, the Department of Excise, the Sri Lanka Navy and the Sri Lanka Police undertake enforcement and surveillance activities, including inspections, investigations, raids and intelligence-sharing, to prevent illegal importation, distribution, and sale of illicit cigarettes. Those agencies work in coordination with each other to combat the illicit tobacco trade and to ensure compliance with the applicable laws and regulations.\n\n5. Amendments to the Tobacco Tax Act are currently being drafted with the objective of strengthening tax administration and improving compliance. I just want to inform you that currently they are at the Legal Draftsman\u2019s Department.\n\nTo date, approximately, there are over 700 registered beedi manufacturers who have obtained licences from the Excise Department. The Department is currently taking steps to identify small-scale beedi manufacturers and create a necessary framework to enable them to market their products through formal channels.\n\nThe Government recognizes the importance of safeguarding the livelihoods of genuine small-scale beedi workers while advancing national public health objectives. The raids are conducted with the assistance of the Sri Lanka Navy to prevent the illegal importation of raw materials used in beedi manufacturing. Measures have also been taken to restrict the purchase and use of\n\n1941 1942\n\n\u0db4\u0dca\u0dad\u0db8 \u0dc3\u0dc1\u0db8\u0dca\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\nillegally imported beedi raw materials. Accordingly, beedi manufacturers are now required and have been instructed to procure raw materials only from registered importers."
