    "chunk_text": "Investigations into the Easter Sunday attacks also remain an important national responsibility. The victim families are still waiting for answers. Therefore, the Government has a duty to ensure that every credible lead is followed, every evidence is examined into and no political considerations stand between the victims and the truth. We should neither predetermine conclusions nor allow serious unanswered questions to disappear with time. Accountability must come through credible investigation and proper administration of justice.\n\n129 130\n\n\u0db4\u0db4\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2 \u0db8\u0dca\u0dca \u0daf\n\nsomething more sustainable. Sri Lanka recorded a real GDP growth of 5 per cent in 2025, while the IMF said, in May, 2026, that the programme performance remained generally strong and that all the quantitative performance criteria had been met by end-December, 2025. Stability is important, but stability is not development.\n\nOur next phase must be production, investments, exports, employment, technology and greater integration with the global economy. In 2025, Sri Lanka achieved a significant milestone in economic recovery; I repeat, in 2025, Sri Lanka achieved a significant milestone in economic recovery. Our merchandise exports reached approximately US Dollars 13.6 billion, the highest annual merchandise export earnings ever recorded in the country\u2019s history. With merchandise exports and selected service exports considered together, Sri Lanka\u2019s total export earnings exceeded US Dollars 17.2 billion. That growth was not confined to one sector. Our apparel and textile sector continued to remain the largest export contributor, generating nearly US Dollars 4.9 billion. Tea exports exceeded US Dollars 1.5 billion, while coconut-based exports recorded an exceptional growth, reaching more than US Dollars 1.2 billion."
