    "chunk_size_bytes": 1474,
    "chunk_text": "The UK Government has announced a landmark proposal to ban social media use by children under 16, requiring platforms to enforce a minimum age of 16. The measure would be supported through the Children\u2019s wellbeing and Schools Bill, alongside provisions in the Online Safety Act, aimed at strengthening online protections for young people. The ban will restrict access on major platforms like Instagram, TikTok, YouTube, Snapchat, Facebook and X - Twitter, while exempting messaging apps like WhatsApp. Australia\u2019s landmark Online Safety Amendment (Social Media Minimum Age) Act bans children under 16 from holding accounts on major social medial platforms.\n\nSir, I am very conscious of the fact that social media has destroyed the mindset of some of our children and have subjected them to various cybercrimes. I have already handed over a Private Members\u2019 Bill dealing with social media minimum age. I also spoke to the Minister of Justice and National Integration, and he said that the Legal Draftsman is in the process of drafting a Bill. I do not want to take credit for that. If the Government could expedite the process and bring in an Act, that would solve this major social issue.\n\nSir, the Social Media Minimum Age Act should only be to protect children in Sri Lanka from harmful online use, not to be anti-technology. It is acknowledged that digital tools have real value, but it is also argued that children need protection from online harm. I would like to"
