    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24313_english_2026-09-08.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-08-2026-09-08-0216",
    "chunk_index": 216,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "768b042de24e40a35a6b0f3aa932c314",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1621,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, the Social Media Minimum Age Act should only be to protect children in Sri Lanka from harmful online use, not to be anti-technology. It is acknowledged that digital tools have real value, but it is also argued that children need protection from online harm. I would like to\n\nAlso, if you look at online violence, 28 per cent of the children who use the Internet have experienced some form of online violence. You know that various indecent messages are sent online, and 28 per cent of our children have been subject to such indecent messages while 26 per cent of the children are subject to indecent links and advertisements and 27 per cent have been subject to cyberbullying and extortion. Nearly 20 per cent, that is one in five, have reported that indecent images of themselves were shared online. Unfortunately, today, our children get friendly with various people online and sometimes, share their private photographs, some of which are compromising. Those photographs are shared online and some children had committed suicide due to that. Ninety two per cent of the victimized children would not seek legal redress. That means, unfortunately, children who are subject to online issues, especially where various private images and conversations have been shared online, which is an offence, do not report them.\n\nThen, Sri Lanka ranks 20th globally for cyberspace-related threats. There are 9.1 million distinct cyber threats detected in a single year. Sir, children's sexual abuse material availability rate is 27.8 per 10,000 people, which is a very dangerous phenomena. There is a lot of data on these issues."
  },
  {
