    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "77e104fe323e27e830dcc200a457fbec",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1586,
    "chunk_text": "Hon. Speaker and Hon. Members of the House, there is the woman living in Colombo and the woman living in a remote village. They are not a homogenous group. Among them, there is the plantation worker, the factory worker, the professional, the entrepreneur, the woman working abroad to sustain her family, the single mother, the elderly woman and the woman living with a disability. And, there are women who, because of poverty, social circumstance and other vulnerabilities, may not even know where to turn to when their rights are violated. If this legislation is to achieve its full purpose, the benefits must reach each one of them. We must be unequivocal in this matter.\n\nWe must also know that harassment of women is not a minor inconvenience, and discrimination is not something that a woman should be told just to tolerate, as they have been told through ages.\n\nSir, there is another dimension of empowerment that requires our particular attention, which is economic empowerment. A woman who has no economic independence can be extremely vulnerable. Economic empowerment means access to education, employment, finance and entrepreneurship. It also means, fair\n\nremuneration, ability to own and control resources and ensures that work of equal value receives equal remuneration.\n\nThe Act also recognizes particular needs of women with disabilities. We must, therefore, ensure that women's empowerment is not only reflected in our economic policies and employment policies, but also in our financial inclusion programmes and in national development strategies that we may formulate."
  },
